itary action must be carried out by the armed forces of Russia. Actions carried out by private military contractors, mercenaries, proxy forces, or the armed forces of any other state, including Belarus, will not qualify.\\n\\nAn \\\"EU member state\\\" refers to any of the 27 member states of the European Union as of market creation: Austria, Belgium, Bulgaria, Croatia, Cyprus, Czechia, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Greece, Hungary, Ireland, Italy, Latvia, Lithuania, Luxembourg, Malta, Netherlands, Poland, Portugal, Romania, Slovakia, Slovenia, Spain, and Sweden.\\n\\nThe following actions do not constitute a qualifying military action:\\nMunitions destroyed or intercepted before impact;\\nSurface-to-air missile strikes;\\nSmall-arms fire;\\nGround incursions;\\nCyber operations;\\nNaval gunfire and artillery fire;\\nHowitzers, artillery pieces, mortars, and rocket artillery (e.g. MLRS systems);\\nMinor surface-to-surface strikes, including short range loitering munitions, FPV drones, and ATGM strikes;\\nSabotage, arson, or other covert acts not carried out with the munitions described above;\\nAny threat, authorization, or announcement of force that has not been executed.\\n\\nAny munition that is intercepted or destroyed before impact does not constitute a qualifying military action. Debris, fragments, or any wreckage from intercepted munitions that land on an EU member state do not constitute a military action regardless of any damage incurred.\\n\\nMunitions that enter the territory of an EU member state in the course of a Russian attack on a non-EU country, including Ukraine, will not qualify unless a consensus of information available from the resolution sources indicates that the munition was directed at a target within the EU member state. A strike on an EU member state that is claimed by the Russian government or military will qualify.\\n\\nThe territory of an EU member state refers to its terrestrial territory, including its internal waters. 